# Line-ending policy for ServerPackCreator.
#
# Why this file exists: the repo is uniformly LF, but nothing enforced it. A contributor with
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default in some setups) would commit CRLF shell scripts, and a CRLF
# `start.sh` fails at runtime with a "bad interpreter" error.
#
# Note what this file does NOT decide: the endings of a *generated* server pack. The templates under
# `serverpackcreator-api/src/main/resources/de/griefed/resources/server_files/` are normalised at
# generation time — `ServerPackProvisioner.lineEndingFor` picks the convention from the written file's
# extension — so whatever an operator's editor did to their copy of a template never reaches the pack.
# That rule is pinned by `RunFileLineEndingsTest`; this file only governs the working tree.
#
# Rule of thumb: LF everywhere, CRLF only where Windows genuinely requires it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default: treat as text, store LF, check out LF on every platform.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Must stay LF — a CRLF here breaks execution, not just the diff.
# Shell and fish scripts are run by an interpreter that chokes on carriage returns, and the SPC start
# templates become the scripts inside generated server packs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.fish      text eol=lf
gradlew     text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Must be CRLF — Windows batch files. cmd.exe resolves a `goto`/`call` label by scanning the file, and
# misses a label crossing a 512-byte boundary when no carriage return has reset its line counter, so an
# LF-only .bat jumps into the wrong place or reports a label it can see as missing. This covers Gradle's
# wrapper scripts. PowerShell is happy with LF, so .ps1 stays on the LF default.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# Exception: the start-script *templates* stay LF, and it costs nothing — generation decides the endings
# of what it writes, so a template's own endings never reach a user. Keeping them LF leaves that one
# directory uniform and its diffs readable. Scoped to the directory so a future .bat template inherits
# the same rule.
# (Later rules win in .gitattributes, so this must stay below the `*.bat` line above.)
serverpackcreator-api/src/main/resources/de/griefed/resources/server_files/*.bat text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tool-owned: left byte-for-byte alone so install4j can rewrite it without git reformatting it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
spc.install4j -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary — never touch. git's auto-detection already gets these right, but declaring them means a new
# fixture (the test jars, the icons, the sample modpack zip) can never be corrupted by normalisation.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.webp      binary
*.ico       binary
*.icns      binary
*.xcf       binary
*.jar       binary
*.zip       binary
*.gz        binary
*.exe       binary
*.dll       binary
*.so        binary
*.dylib     binary
*.ttf       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.pdf       binary
